Source: MimeKit UnitTests/Cryptography/ArcSignerTests.cs and
ArcVerifierTests.cs (license in LICENSE). The named signer inputs and public
test key dummy.pem come from the corresponding MimeKit methods. Keep their
wire whitespace. In particular, "Hey gang,  " in c_simple_simple.eml has two
significant trailing spaces.

The verifier corpus predates the final ARC syntax. For example, MimeKit's
cv_pass_i1_1 passes with i= after other AS/AMS tags and with AAR signed by
h=. RFC 8617 sections 4.1.2 and 4.1.3 require a leading instance; section
4.1.2 prohibits ARC headers in h=. draft-cv_pass_i1_1.eml preserves this
upstream message verbatim (CRLF wire endings) as a documented difference.
Blindly importing its negative variants would fail at the wrong check.

generate.py uses Python cryptography (not GMime) and the MimeKit test key to
produce final-RFC versions of the chain and grammar cases. The simple/simple
body hash is the upstream signer oracle. The latest AMS signs the current
body, while each seal authenticates all preceding sets; the old-body-changed
and old-seal-invalid fixtures distinguish those rules. Even malformed signed
tag lists have matching signatures so the test exercises the grammar check.
Regeneration is optional; the test reads the checked-in messages offline.
